The paper claims efficiency—not state-of-the-art accuracy—and reports a substantial 45% memory reduction at matched accuracy against the standard open baseline. It should therefore be evaluated on whether:

- the baseline and accuracy-matching protocol are appropriate and fair;
- memory savings are measured rigorously, including relevant training/inference conditions;
- runtime, hardware, model size, and other tradeoffs are disclosed;
- the comparison covers sufficiently strong reproducible open methods;
- the efficiency gain is technically novel and practically meaningful.

A closed model may be discussed as context, but it is generally unsuitable as a decisive benchmark when its implementation, training data, resource use, or evaluation details cannot be controlled or reproduced. Rejection would be justified only if the paper’s framing implicitly requires frontier accuracy, its chosen baseline is obsolete or selectively weak, or the memory gain disappears under fair comparisons—not merely because a newer closed system is more accurate.

